From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:55:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: let an ARM64EC FlushInstructionCache reach the
 emulator

clang defines __x86_64__ as well as __arm64ec__ for an ARM64EC target, so
the ARM64EC kernelbase took the x86-64 FlushInstructionCache, which
returns TRUE without a syscall (`mov w0, #1; ret` in the built object).
The ARM64EC branch below it, with its wrapper that keeps RDX, was never
built. FEX then learnt of code an x86-64 program rewrote in place only
from its write trap. On iOS that trap misses a code page whose 16 KiB
host page also holds a writable page: the childabort probe's flush phase
returned the value written before on 980,469 of 996,036 calls in that
layout, though it flushed after every rewrite.

The x86-64 branch now excludes ARM64EC, which takes the ARM64EC branch
and calls NtFlushInstructionCache (BTCpu64FlushInstructionCache), as
0039 does for i386. Built for the first time, that branch had two faults
of its own:
- the naked wrapper failed to assemble ("Incorrect size for
  #FlushInstructionCache prologue"): its store of x1 sat inside the SEH
  prologue with no unwind directive. The store now follows
  .seh_endprologue; x1 is volatile, so the unwinder need not know of it.
- the static flush_instruction_cache, called only from that asm, was
  dropped as unused, and the link failed on "#flush_instruction_cache".
  It is now __attribute__((used)), as ntdll's functions called from asm are.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-13-arm64ec-flush.md
Offered-upstream: no; the branch came with a Valve pick (wine-valve) and any ARM64EC build of it has the same no-op
---
 dlls/kernelbase/memory.c | 9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/kernelbase/memory.c b/dlls/kernelbase/memory.c
index e6eec6d2914..cebbc205ed6 100644
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/memory.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/memory.c
@@ -142,14 +142,17 @@ BOOL WINAPI DECLSPEC_HOTPATCH FlushInstructionCache( HANDLE process, LPCVOID add
 {
     return set_ntstatus( NtFlushInstructionCache( process, addr, size ));
 }
-#elif defined(__x86_64__)
+#elif defined(__x86_64__) && !defined(__arm64ec__)
 BOOL WINAPI DECLSPEC_HOTPATCH FlushInstructionCache( HANDLE process, LPCVOID addr, SIZE_T size )
 {
     /* X86 processors have coherent instruction and data caches, no need to do anything */
     return TRUE;
 }
 #else
-static BOOL flush_instruction_cache( HANDLE process, LPCVOID addr, SIZE_T size )
+/* ARM64EC comes here too: clang defines __x86_64__ for it as well, but its
+ * x86-64 code runs under the emulator, which learns of code rewritten in place
+ * from NtFlushInstructionCache (BTCpu64FlushInstructionCache). */
+static BOOL __attribute__((used)) flush_instruction_cache( HANDLE process, LPCVOID addr, SIZE_T size )
 {
     CROSS_PROCESS_WORK_LIST *list;
 
@@ -167,9 +170,9 @@ BOOL WINAPI __attribute__((naked)) FlushInstructionCache( HANDLE process, LPCVOI
 {
     asm( ".seh_proc \"#FlushInstructionCache\"\n\t"
          "stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!\n\t"
-         "str x1, [sp, #16]\n\t"
          ".seh_save_fplr_x 32\n\t"
          ".seh_endprologue\n\t"
+         "str x1, [sp, #16]\n\t"
          "bl \"#flush_instruction_cache\"\n\t"
          "ldr x1, [sp, #16]\n\t"
          "ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32\n\t"
